            "text": "{\n  \"persuasion\": \"PERSUADED\",\n  \"support_scope\": \"FULL\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The glossary entry asserts a strictly lexical and translational fact: the English word 'hell' (particularly in the KJV, as shown in the evidence) is used to translate distinct Hebrew and Greek terms (Sheol, Hades, Gehenna, Tartarus) which have different referents. The scriptural evidence explicitly demonstrates this conflation, using the same English word for the grave (Psalm 16:10; Acts 2:27), final judgment (Matthew 10:28), and a holding place for fallen angels (2 Peter 2:4). The common interpretation itself acknowledges this lexical distinction as a standard fact of biblical studies. The chapter's broader, highly contested theological arguments for annihilationism, soul sleep, and the ontology of the fire are completely excluded from the glossary entry and are therefore preserved as chapter-only objections.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The English word 'hell' is an ambiguous translation label that conflates distinct underlying Hebrew and Greek words (Sheol, Hades, Gehenna, Tartarus) which do not share a single scriptural referent.\",\n  \"book_secondary_elaborations\": [\n    \"Its meaning must be taken from the underlying Hebrew or Greek word.\",\n    \"It translates Sheol, Hades, Gehenna, and tartaroō.\"\n  ],\n  \"unsupported_glossary_assertions\": [],\n  \"chapter_only_objections\": [\n    \"The chapter argues for conditional immortality/annihilationism (that the wicked are permanently destroyed/un-created rather than eternally tormented).\",\n    \"The chapter argues for soul sleep (that the dead are completely unconscious in the grave).\",\n    \"The chapter argues that heaven is not the destination of the saved, but rather the renewed earth.\",\n    \"The chapter argues that the 'fire' of hell is not a place, but the unshielded presence of God Himself.\",\n    \"The chapter dismisses the rich man and Lazarus (Luke 16) as merely a parable about the sufficiency of Scripture rather than conveying literal afterlife realities.\"\n  ],\n  \"strongest_counter_interpretation\": \"The English word 'hell' fundamentally conceptualizes the postmortem state of judgment. While older Bibles historically translated multiple Greek and Hebrew words with it, modern theology and translation give it a primary theological referent (Gehenna/final punishment), making it a unified doctrinal concept rather than just an ambiguous label.\",\n  \"counter_core_identification\": \"'Hell' is a unified theological concept denoting postmortem punishment, which properly corresponds to Gehenna, even if older translations applied the word broadly to the grave.\",\n  \"counter_relation_to_book_core\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"material_core_denial\": \"Denies that 'hell' has no single scriptural referent, arguing instead that its primary referent is Gehenna (final judgment) and that historical translation imprecision does not negate the unified theological concept.\",\n  \"counter_case\": [\n    \"In modern usage and many modern translations, 'hell' is reserved almost exclusively for Gehenna, while Sheol and Hades are transliterated or translated as 'the grave'.\",\n    \"The systematic theological concept of 'hell' maps directly to the Lake of Fire/Gehenna, giving it a single doctrinal referent regardless of translation history.\"\n  ],\n  \"book_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Accurately explains the text of the KJV and older translations, which strictly use 'hell' across four distinct Greek/Hebrew words.\",\n    \"Forces the reader to distinguish the final judgment (Gehenna) from passages concerning the intermediate state or grave (Sheol/Hades), such as Acts 2:27.\",\n    \"Aligns flawlessly with standard academic consensus on the lexical distinctions of the underlying terms.\"\n  ],\n  \"counter_explanatory_advantages\": [\n    \"Reflects the trajectory of modern English translations which attempt to fix the ambiguity by reserving 'hell' for Gehenna.\",\n    \"Captures the common systematic-theology usage of the term in everyday Christian discourse.\"\n  ],\n  \"evidence_book_core_cannot_explain\": [],\n  \"comparative_winner\": \"BOOK\",\n  \"decisive_sources\": [\n    \"Psalm 16:10\",\n    \"Acts 2:27\",\n    \"Matthew 10:28\",\n    \"2 Peter 2:4\"\n  ],\n  \"unresolved_objections\": [],\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
